From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 22:48:56 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 33/37] arm64: dts: apple: t6050: describe the Neural Engine in
 a SoC-level dtsi

t6050-j714s-ane.dtsi has nothing J714s-specific in it: the ane0 node, its
three DARTs and ANE_SYS are in t6050-peripherals.dtsi, and the two preload
reservations are placeholders that the boot loader fills from the ADT of the
machine it boots (aura matches the node by its "apple,t6050-ane"
compatible, on any board). The accel/ane driver also matches only the
compatible and refuses a hand-off whose preloaded firmware it does not
know.

Move the content to t6050-ane.dtsi. The new file also holds a vote on
ANE_SYS's parents in the ADT power tree, FAB8_SOC and FAB9_SOC, and on
FAB8_SOC's parent FAB0_PIOGW, as firmware-owned domains. Without them
ANE_SYS cannot register on a board that does not describe its Secure
Enclave or camera, which today are the only users that enable those
fabrics. J714s already enables all three with the same properties.

t6050-j714s-ane.dtsi stays as a one-line include for board files that
still name it; t6050-ane.dtsi has an include guard, so a board that gets
both is fine.

The ANE description is DjDeveloperr's, from commit 648384409c69
("arm64: dts: apple: t6050: Describe the ANE on the T8140 model, enable it
on J714s"); this only changes where it lives.

J714s now includes t6050-ane.dtsi directly. Its DTB is byte-identical
before and after, and no other board changes in this commit.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>

[re-cut onto the K114s r3d tree]
